  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Occasional rain mainly north of Bangor the rest of this\nafternoon, tapering off this evening.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nRain totals in general have been more than expected over\nnorth/northwest areas, and less Downeast. Totals are approaching\nan inch around Presque Isle, and it's still raining over most\nareas north of Bangor. Downeast totals have generally been from\nbarely measurable to as much as two tenths of an inch. \nGenerally, look for a decreasing trend in the rain later this \nafternoon, with mostly dry conditions tonight. Anticipate enough\nof a breeze from the northeast to keep fog minimal. A few \nshowers are still possible mainly in Aroostook County during the\nday Friday, but nothing that significant.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Showers, and potentially a few thunderstorms,\nlikely late this weekend into early next week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA cold front approaches our area Saturday night with an area of\nlow pressure developing along it Sunday. The low should pass \nnorth of our area Sunday night into Monday. Showers and an \nisolated thunderstorm possible over the North Woods late \nSaturday as the cold front approaches with more showers and a \ngreater chance for thunderstorms Sunday as low pressure passes \nto our north. The greatest chance for storms will be over \nnorthern and western Maine as southerly flow is likely to keep \nstable marine air over more southern and eastern areas. Models \nhave started to signal at a delay in the cold front as well as \nweaker instability, which would help limit thunder development \nin these showers. Steadier rain from the front will overtake the\narea Sunday night into Monday, though totals should be less \nthan half an inch, with some locally higher amounts in heavier \nshowers.",
